id: opencode
name: OpenCode

Add Birks AI as a custom OpenAI-compatible provider in OpenCode's config.

--- file ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json (json) ---
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "birks-ai": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "Birks AI",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://ai.birks.dev/v1",
        "apiKey": "{env:BIRKS_AI_API_KEY}"
      },
      "models": {
        "Qwen3-Coder-30B": {
          "name": "Qwen3-Coder-30B"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

--- commands (bash) ---
export BIRKS_AI_API_KEY="YOUR_API_KEY"

opencode

--- notes ---
- The {env:BIRKS_AI_API_KEY} placeholder reads the credential from your environment at run time.
- OpenCode's provider schema has changed across releases. If this does not load, check the current docs at opencode.ai and report it so the snippet can be updated.
- A project-local opencode.json overrides the global one, which is useful for trying this on a single repository first.

--- agent prompt ---
Configure OpenCode on this machine to use my self-hosted Birks AI endpoint at https://ai.birks.dev. Add a custom openai-compatible provider to my OpenCode config, reading the API key from the environment rather than hardcoding it. Use the model "Qwen3-Coder-30B". Read the API key from the BIRKS_AI_API_KEY environment variable. Do not write the key itself into any file in a git repository, and do not print it. Preserve my existing unrelated configuration.
